description:
The NodeSearchPanel has a useEffect at line 84 that subscribes to [query, nodes] but also modifies nodes within the effect body:

```
useEffect(() => {
  const searchResults = searchNodes(nodes, query);
  setResults(searchResults);
  setSelectedIndex(0);

  if (query.trim()) {
    const matchingIds = searchResults.map((r) => r.node.id);
    const updatedNodes = nodes.map((n) => ({
      ...n,
      selected: matchingIds.includes(n.id),
    }));
    useGraphStore.setState({ nodes: updatedNodes });
  }
}, [query, nodes]);
```

Problem: When query has text, the effect calls useGraphStore.setState({ nodes: updatedNodes }). Since .map() always creates a new array reference, Zustand considers the state changed, which triggers the component to re-render with the new nodes reference, which re-triggers the effect, which calls setState again — creating an infinite loop.

In practice, React 18's batching may limit visible damage, but this causes: (1) doubled state updates on every node drag while search is open, (2) CPU waste from running searchNodes() repeatedly with no actual query change, (3) potential for visible lag when dragging nodes with an active search query.

Reproduction:
1. Open a stream with several nodes
2. Press Ctrl+F to open search
3. Type a search query
4. Drag any node — observe that every position update triggers the search effect twice

Fix options (recommended: option 1):
1. Split into two effects — one for query changes (compute results + highlight), one for node changes (only re-run search if query hasn't changed):
```tsx
// Only update selection when query changes
useEffect(() => {
  if (!query.trim()) return;
  const searchResults = searchNodes(nodes, query);
  setResults(searchResults);
  setSelectedIndex(0);
  const matchingIds = searchResults.map((r) => r.node.id);
  const updatedNodes = nodes.map((n) => ({
    ...n,
    selected: matchingIds.includes(n.id),
  }));
  useGraphStore.setState({ nodes: updatedNodes });
  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, [query]); // Only re-run on query change, not nodes change
```

2. Alternative: Use a ref to track whether the setState was caused by this effect (skip re-running if so):
```tsx
const selfUpdateRef = useRef(false);
useEffect(() => {
  if (selfUpdateRef.current) { selfUpdateRef.current = false; return; }
  // ... existing logic
  selfUpdateRef.current = true;
  useGraphStore.setState({ nodes: updatedNodes });
}, [query, nodes]);
```

Option 1 is cleaner. The tradeoff is that search results won't auto-update if a node's text changes while search is open — acceptable since users don't typically edit and search simultaneously.
